(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cp-exam-2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which bone marrow neoplasm is defined by ineffective hematopoiesis with cytopenias and dysplasia?","choices":["Myelodysplastic neoplasm","Myeloproliferative neoplasm","Acute myeloid leukemia","Plasma-cell myeloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myelodysplastic neoplasms (the World Health Organization's term; myelodysplastic syndromes remains in use elsewhere) show ineffective hematopoiesis with cytopenias and dysplasia, and they can progress to acute myeloid leukemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterizes the arteries?","choices":["Low pressure, thin walls, and a role as a blood reservoir","High pressure, strong walls, high flow velocity","Control conduits releasing blood into the capillaries","Collection of blood from capillaries into larger vessels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the contrast with veins is instructive: veins are low pressure with thin walls and serve as the major reservoir of extra blood. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What forms the hyaline membranes of acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Inflammatory deposition of proteins","Collagen from proliferating fibroblasts","Calcified immune complexes","Inhaled inorganic dust"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In acute respiratory distress syndrome, hyaline membranes are associated with inflammatory deposition of proteins. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which are listed as PROBABLE rather than major risk factors?","choices":["Male sex, homocysteine and high sensitivity C reactive protein","Hypertension, diabetes and obesity","Age and family history","Abnormal lipids and cigarette smoking"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Male sex, homocysteine and high sensitivity C reactive protein are the probable risk factors, a separate category from the major ones: age, family history, abnormal lipids, cigarette smoking, hypertension, diabetes mellitus and obesity. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which conditions predispose to aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Varicose veins and hemorrhoids","Raynaud phenomenon and cold exposure","Atherosclerosis, hypertension and smoking","Thrombocytosis and dehydration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerosis, hypertension and smoking are the predisposing conditions for aortic aneurysm, a localized abnormal dilation that follows compromise in the structure or function of the wall's connective tissue. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which antibody fragment carries the effector functions?","choices":["Fc","Fab","Light-chain variable region","Hinge region"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fc contains the heavy-chain constant regions involved in effector functions; the complement-binding site lies on its CH2 domain. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 41, 42"},{"q":"What produces adhesive mediastinopericarditis?","choices":["Fluid accumulating without inflammation","A dense, non-elastic sac encasing the heart","Immune cross-reactivity after streptococcal infection","Suppurative or caseous pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cardiac workload rises because the heart is now working against the structures it is stuck to. Or surgery, destroying the sac so the heart adheres to surrounding structures. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Under the World Health Organization 2024 cutoffs (ages 15 to 65), below what hemoglobin is a nonpregnant woman anemic?","choices":["10 g/dL","11 g/dL","12 g/dL","13 g/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The World Health Organization 2024 cutoffs for ages 15 to 65 are a hemoglobin below 13 g/dL in men and below 12 g/dL in nonpregnant women. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Where does the airway close in obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Small bronchioles","Collapsible segments of the pharynx","The alveolar ducts","The trachea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea-hypopnea syndrome is complete or partial closure of the collapsible segments of the pharynx during sleep. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 100, 101"},{"q":"What defines the restrictive lung diseases?","choices":["Increased airflow resistance","Reversible airway inflammation","Decreased expansion of the lungs","Dilation of the bronchi"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive lung diseases are manifested by decreased expansion of the lungs due to alterations in the parenchyma, pleura, chest wall or neuromuscular function. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which is an example of NATURAL passive immunity?","choices":["Rabies immune globulin given after exposure","Antibodies made after a natural infection","Maternal IgG crossing the placenta","A vaccine series that builds antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Natural passive immunity is maternal IgG through the placenta and IgA in breast milk: preformed antibodies from another source, transferred naturally. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What produces the purulent, foul-smelling sputum of bronchiectasis?","choices":["Mast cells release leukotrienes","Immune complexes fill the alveoli","Chyle leaks into the pleura","Dilated airways act as infection pockets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchiolar dilatations serve as pockets of infection, producing purulent, foul-smelling sputum; Haemophilus influenzae is the most common bacterial cause. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What does plasmin do?","choices":["Activates factor X","Crosslinks fibrin","Degrades fibrin","Converts fibrinogen to fibrin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In fibrinolysis, plasminogen becomes plasmin, which degrades fibrin; this is separate from the regulation of coagulation by antithrombin and activated protein C. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"What regulates vascular resistance?","choices":["The capillaries, by altering their permeability","The arterioles, by neural and hormonal input","The veins, by varying their capacitance","The adventitia, by contracting around the vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is described as a delicate balance between vasoconstrictors and vasodilators rather than a single controlling signal. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which mast-cell mediator in allergic asthma is the slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis?","choices":["Histamine","Serotonin","Bradykinin","Leukotrienes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mast cells release histamine, slow-reacting substances of anaphylaxis (leukotrienes), prostaglandins, bradykinins, eosinophilic chemotactic factor and serotonin. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does an infarcted area change over the weeks that follow?","choices":["It pales, softens, then scars by six weeks","It is replaced by functioning muscle within two weeks","It calcifies within forty-eight hours","It remains unchanged indefinitely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The area becomes paler within 18 to 24 hours, then turns yellowish and soft with a border of red vascular connective tissue. Necrotic tissue is taken away by one to two weeks, and tough fibrous scar has formed by six weeks. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the healthy adult, where does almost all hematopoiesis occur?","choices":["Axial skeleton, proximal humerus and femur","Distal radius, tibia and the small hand bones","The thymus and the peripheral lymph nodes","The spleen and the liver sinusoids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most hematopoiesis occurs in the bone marrow, and in the healthy adult almost all of it is in the axial skeleton plus the proximal humerus and femur. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of these organs belongs to the lymphatic system?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland","Pancreas","Thymus gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The labeled lymphatic system includes the thymus gland together with the tonsils, spleen, appendix, lymph vessels and the lymph nodes of the neck, axilla, groin and knee. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does somatic hypermutation in germinal centers produce after antigen activation?","choices":["Immunoglobulin gene rearrangement","Maturation arrest","Affinity maturation","Proof of a malignant clone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After antigen activation, B cells in germinal centers can undergo somatic hypermutation, and selection of the better-binding cells produces affinity maturation. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does the heart compensate well for a slowly progressive stenosis?","choices":["It dilates the affected chamber immediately","It hypertrophies its myocardial cells as the gradient develops","It develops collateral circulation across the valve","It reduces the heart rate permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow progression allows the adaptation, which is why such valves can be severely narrowed before anything is noticed. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why does atrial fibrillation develop in mitral stenosis?","choices":["The ventricle hypertrophies and compresses the conducting system","Coronary ischemia damages the sinus node","The atrium enlarges as its volume rises","Pericardial inflammation spreads to the atrium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the enlarged fibrillating atrium then predisposes to clot within it, which is the next consequence in the chain. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which two features classify the lymphoid neoplasms?","choices":["Blood count and marrow cellularity","Clonality and the white count","Patient age and cell size","Cell lineage and stage of maturation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoid neoplasms are classified by cell lineage and stage of maturation: precursor B or T cells, mature B cells, mature T and natural killer cells, and germinal-center B cells for classic Hodgkin lymphoma. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Microcytic hypochromic anemias are caused by disorders of what?","choices":["Erythroid precursor maturation","Hemoglobin synthesis","Red-cell membrane structure","Red-cell survival in the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Microcytic hypochromic anemias are caused by disorders of hemoglobin synthesis, which is why iron deficiency, thalassemia and sideroblastic anemia sit in the microcytic group. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"How do antibodies bring about lysis of a target cell?","choices":["By releasing perforin themselves","By recruiting the complement system","By cross-linking soluble antigen","By coating the cell for phagocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lysis, destruction of the cell, happens through incorporation with the complement system. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which surface marker identifies natural killer cells?","choices":["CD3","CD4","CD19","CD56"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Natural killer cells carry CD56, and many also carry CD16, which binds the Fc region of IgG. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 21, 34"},{"q":"Why does loss of lung tissue raise airway resistance in emphysema?","choices":["Mucus plugs fill the bronchial lumen","Bronchial smooth muscle contracts","Fibrosis stiffens the airway walls","Radial traction holding airways open is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loss of lung tissue removes the radial traction that holds the airways open, increasing airway resistance and decreasing airflow; trapped air distends air sacs into bullae. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which cells actually produce antibodies?","choices":["Naive B cells","Helper T cells","Plasma cells","Memory B cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In humoral immunity B cells are transformed into plasma cells, which are the actual antibody-producing cells. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 22, 24"},{"q":"At what point does myocardium become ischemic?","choices":["When metabolic demand for oxygen exceeds supply","When coronary blood flow falls below a fixed absolute value","Only once a coronary artery is completely occluded","Only when the patient experiences chest pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a relationship rather than a threshold, which is why ischemia can arise either from supply falling or from demand rising with the supply unchanged. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why is a patient with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy symptomatic?","choices":["Ventricular outflow obstruction and impaired diastolic filling","Reduced contractility of a thin, dilated ventricle","Fibrosis restricting filling","Backflow across an incompetent aortic valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The thickened muscle obstructs the way out and stiffens the chamber, so both halves of the cycle suffer. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How do cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells?","choices":["Complement pores cause osmotic lysis","Perforin and granzymes trigger apoptosis","Antibody coating speeds their ingestion","Blocking the virus from attaching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Perforin release is important to the effector function, and perforin with granzymes helps trigger target-cell apoptosis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 25, 31"},{"q":"What is the hemodynamic consequence of aortic stenosis?","choices":["Obstruction to filling during diastole","Backflow into the ventricle during diastole","Backflow into the atrium during systole","Obstruction to outflow during systole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The aortic valve is open during systole, so this is when the obstruction bites. With a pressure gradient across the valve during ejection. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How does the Sepsis-3 definition describe sepsis?","choices":["Immature bands above 10 percent of white cells","Neutrophilia during acute bacterial inflammation","Neutropenia below 500 cells/µL with infection","Life-threatening organ dysfunction from infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sepsis-3 defines sepsis as infection-associated, life-threatening organ dysfunction; the band count above 10 percent was a historical criterion of the older systemic inflammatory response syndrome definition. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which features describe idiopathic pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Slowly progressive, commoner in men","Rapidly progressive, commoner in women","Results from a known disease process","Usually begins after the seventh decade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic pulmonary hypertension is rapidly progressive, more common in women than men, of unknown cause, and appears in the third to fourth decade. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What makes up a basic antibody unit?","choices":["One heavy and one light chain","Four identical heavy chains","Two heavy and two light chains","Two heavy chains alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A basic antibody unit has two heavy chains and two light chains, connected by disulfide bonds. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does the pulmonary loop do?","choices":["Carries oxygenated blood from the left heart to the tissues","Returns deoxygenated blood from the tissues to the right heart","Supplies the heart muscle itself with blood","Carries deoxygenated blood from the right heart to the lungs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary loop pumps deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs for oxygenation. The systemic loop is its mirror image, carrying highly oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to the body tissues. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which are the four principal mechanisms of vascular disease?","choices":["Inflammation, infection, infarction and ischemia","Hypertrophy, atrophy, metaplasia and dysplasia","Thrombosis, embolism, stasis and reflux","Weakening, narrowing, dilation and rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and narrowing itself can be progressive, as in atherosclerosis, or precipitous, as in thrombosis or embolism. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What appearance do vessels affected by fibromuscular dysplasia have on angiography?","choices":["A smooth tapering narrowing","A string of beads","A blood-filled false channel alongside the true lumen","A saccular outpouching from one wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Segments of the wall are focally thickened by hyperplasia and fibrosis of the media and intima, giving the vessel a string of beads appearance on angiography. The renal arteries are an example. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What distinguishes primary from secondary immunodeficiency?","choices":["Primary is caused by medication","Primary follows a malignancy","Primary is intrinsic, often genetic","Primary arises from poor nutrition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary immunodeficiency is an intrinsic defect in immune development or function, often with a genetic basis; secondary immunodeficiency is reduced function from disease, medications, malignancy or inadequate nutrition. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 76, 77"},{"q":"Into which cells do activated, selected B cells differentiate?","choices":["T cells or natural killer cells","Lymphoblasts or myeloblasts","Plasma cells or memory B cells","Monocytes or macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After antigen activation and selection, B cells differentiate into plasma cells (which produce antibody) or memory B cells. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 24"},{"q":"Which alveolar cell secretes surfactant?","choices":["Type II pneumocyte","Type I pneumocyte","Alveolar macrophage","Capillary endothelial cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The type II pneumocyte is the surfactant-secreting cell of the alveolar wall; type I pneumocytes form most of the thin alveolar epithelium. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What happens once the injured endothelium becomes more permeable?","choices":["Calcium is deposited directly into the media","Smooth muscle cells migrate out of the vessel wall","The vessel dilates to preserve flow","Leukocytes are recruited and low density lipoprotein leaks through to be oxidized"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lipid has to get into the wall before it can do harm, and the increased permeability is what lets it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of stable, typical angina?","choices":["Vasospasm occurring unpredictably at rest","Stenotic vessels reduce flow to a critical level","Plaque rupture with acute thrombosis","Inflammation of the pericardium limiting filling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The narrowing is fixed, so it is the rise in demand that precipitates each episode -- which is why the attacks are predictable. So perfusion fails under increased workload. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which organs does leukostasis often involve?","choices":["Liver and spleen","Lungs and brain","Kidneys and bones","Marrow and thymus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leukostasis is impaired microcirculation with organ injury, often involving the lungs or the brain. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What distinguishes very low density lipoprotein from low density lipoprotein?","choices":["It is high in cholesterol and decreases risk","It is high in triglycerides rather than cholesterol","It carries cholesterol back to the liver","It is not associated with atherosclerotic risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both raise risk, so the distinction to hold is what each is carrying rather than which direction it pushes risk. Increases risk. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What commonly starts the classical complement pathway?","choices":["Lectins binding microbial sugars","C3 activating on permissive surfaces","C1 binding antigen-bound IgM or IgG","C5b inserting into a membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The classical pathway commonly begins when C1 recognizes antigen-bound IgM or IgG, though some activation occurs without antibody. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What does gross aspiration deliver to the lower respiratory tract?","choices":["Large numbers of anaerobic bacteria","Inorganic dust particles","Inhaled organic antigens","Blood-borne staphylococci"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gross aspiration can deliver large numbers of anaerobic bacteria to the lower respiratory tract, a major contributor to anaerobic lung infection and abscess formation. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What forms when the vesicle holding an engulfed microbe fuses with a lysosome?","choices":["A phagolysosome","A residual body","A pseudopod","A membrane attack complex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The phagocytic vesicle (phagosome) fuses with a lysosome to form the phagolysosome, where lysosomal enzymes kill and digest the microbe. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is a mechanism of HYPOXIC respiratory failure?","choices":["Increased carbon dioxide production","Inability to sense raised carbon dioxide","Failure to signal effector muscles","Shunt"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxic respiratory failure arises from ventilation/perfusion mismatch, shunt, decreased diffusion, alveolar hypoventilation and high altitude. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 9, 10"},{"q":"How do the chambers compensate in mitral regurgitation?","choices":["The ventricle hypertrophies without dilating","Both atrium and ventricle dilate and hypertrophy to handle the extra volume","The atrium shrinks to limit the regurgitant volume","Neither chamber changes until failure occurs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Volume work produces dilation alongside hypertrophy, as against the hypertrophy alone that pressure work produces. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which change increases net fluid filtration out of a capillary?","choices":["A rise in plasma colloid osmotic pressure","A rise in capillary hydrostatic pressure","A rise in interstitial fluid pressure","A fall in interstitial colloid osmotic pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Net filtration Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pif) − (πc − πif)]: raising capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) pushes more fluid out, as in the effusions of heart failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 17, 93"},{"q":"What is the usual red-cell lifespan that hemolysis shortens?","choices":["About 30 days","About 60 days","About 120 days","About 180 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In hemolytic anemia red-cell survival is shortened below the usual lifespan of about 120 days, hemoglobin breakdown products increase, and a compensating marrow raises reticulocyte production. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which risk factor for atherosclerosis is stated to be the most important?","choices":["Smoking","Hyperlipidemia","Increasing age","Family history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct &mdash; genetics is a strong nonmodifiable risk factor, and family history is stated to be the most important risk factor for atherosclerosis, ahead of modifiable ones such as hyperlipidemia, smoking and hypertension. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which subtypes of small-vessel arteriosclerosis are related to hypertension?","choices":["Fibromuscular and intimal","Medial and adventitial","Hyaline and hyperplastic","Saccular and fusiform"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the vessels involved are the small arteries and arterioles, where downstream ischemic injury is the consequence. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which feature separates asthma from the other obstructive airway diseases?","choices":["Its obstruction is reversible","Its obstruction is persistent","It destroys alveolar walls","It dilates the bronchi for good"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is an obstructive airway disease that is reversible, with airway inflammation and increased airway responsiveness to a variety of stimuli. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 35, 48"},{"q":"What happens during remodeling of a venous thrombus?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages infiltrate the clot","The thrombus dissolves completely with no residual change","The vein wall calcifies along its whole length","The valves regenerate and competence is restored"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, over weeks. The resulting fibrosis decreases blood flow, so the vein does not simply return to normal once the clot has organized. Collagen then replaces the fibrin. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What causes varicose veins?","choices":["Thrombus formation within a deep vein","Inflammation of the vessel wall by immune complexes","Chronic high intraluminal pressure","Direct connection between an artery and a vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, producing dilated tortuous veins. Insufficiency of the venous valves is what allows that pressure to build. The wall support is weakened as well. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What are the three components of the Virchow triad?","choices":["Immobility, dehydration and trauma","Inflammation, infection and infarction","Edema, valve insufficiency and varicose dilation","Wall damage, abnormal blood flow and hypercoagulability"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The abnormal flow may be turbulence or stasis (pooling), and the triggers are multifactorial, with the three involved in varying degrees rather than all being equally present. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Multiple myeloma is a malignancy of which cells?","choices":["Clonal plasma cells","Precursor B cells","Mature B cells","Myeloid progenitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multiple myeloma is a clonal plasma-cell malignancy involving the bone marrow: a malignancy of differentiated, antibody-producing B-lineage cells. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"Which organ produces many of the circulating complement components?","choices":["Bone marrow","Liver","Thymus","Spleen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The liver produces many circulating complement components, and many circulate as inactive precursors until activation starts a regulated cascade. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"By what mechanism does renal artery stenosis raise blood pressure?","choices":["Reduced glomerular flow and afferent pressure induce renin secretion","Direct mechanical transmission of pressure to the aorta","Autonomous aldosterone secretion independent of renin","Loss of nitric oxide production from the renal endothelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and blood volume and vascular tone both rise as a result &mdash; the kidney reads the stenosis as whole-body hypotension and corrects a problem that does not exist. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
